<Layout title="A little curiosity — OpenEngineering"><section class="editorial"><span class="eyebrow">WHY WE’RE HERE</span><h1>The world makes more sense<br/>when you can take it apart.</h1><p class="lede">An engine is a collection of answers to surprisingly simple questions. How do you contain a push? Turn it into a circle? Keep it going?</p><p>OpenEngineering is a free place to follow those questions. You can slow a machine down, see through its walls, and try a change for yourself. Start with a story, then follow your own curiosity.</p><div class="editorial-callout">Understand how machines work.<br/><span>Play with them in 3D.</span></div><h2>Built to be shared.</h2><p>The project began with a love of engineering explainers and a wish that learning could feel more like discovery. GPT-6 Astra helped turn that idea into working exhibits, original explanations, and a reusable foundation.</p><p>The models are simplified teaching tools. We publish sources, explain what is omitted, and welcome corrections. Technical accuracy and a clear explanation matter more than a complicated-looking animation.</p><h2>Open means you can make something with it.</h2><p>Read the source. Reuse an explanation with attribution. Improve a sentence. Build a new exhibit. Our first three machines share the same player so the next contributor can begin with a working example.</p><a class="outline-link" href={path('/contribute/')}>Meet the contribution kit ↗</a><p class="model-note">OpenEngineering is an independent project, unaffiliated with The Open University or its OpenEngineering Laboratory. The working name is still under review.</p></section></Layout>
